[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read by cause, the reports
merged into it are more than one problem, and the queued parser fix leaves the others in place.
Each problem below now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix queued for #4471 does not fix the booking-v3 form hint or the one-day moves.
Do not close their reports when the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

The table is built from `reports-4471.json` and `desk-notes.json` at render time, with the same
cause logic as `causes.ts`. When the desk merges more reports or reads more of them as one-day
moves, the counts follow.

## `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports. Every tenant on file writes the day first.
The shared parser reads `07/04` as 4 July instead of 7 April, so the day and the month trade places:
"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." This is what #4471 describes, and its queued fix is
for this problem.

A few of these reports name no dates (a request for a "day first" setting, "October bookings came
through for January"). They are counted under their tenant's cause, the way `causes.ts` places
every report.

## `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells day-first tenants to type month first

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports, all through the booking form of the
tenants that run `booking-v3`. That form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint
says, month first, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The symptom is the same
swap, so the subject lines match the parser reports and the merge hid it. A parser fix does not
touch the hint; the form has to print the tenant's order, or read the order it asks for.

## `one-day`: bookings move by one day

This covers <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" /> reports: the ones the desk itself read as a
one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). Nothing in them is swapped. The date lands one day off ("Tuesday 6 October showed as
Monday"), and one meeting landed on different days for clients in Lisbon and Toronto.
That points at time zone handling: a date with no time moved through a clock and back. They were
footnoted under `parser` because of their tenant; they need their own fix.

<ProblemReports ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

## Why the merge hid them

- The merge rule matches subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  The subjects say "wrong date", not why, so all three problems share them.
- Intake tags are a guess from the reporter's browser. The table counts how many reports in each
  problem intake tagged as something else.
- A footnote is not a ticket. The one-day moves were noticed and then counted under the wrong cause.

## Lessons

- Merge on cause, not on subject. Read a sample of each big ticket before marking it known.
- When a report does not fit its ticket, give it its own ticket, not a footnote.

<RenderStamp {signature} />
